## Mock vs Spy

## Mock — Replaces the Real Implementation

```typescript
// The real sendEmail function is never called
const sendEmail = jest.fn().mockResolvedValue({ success: true });

const result = await notifyUser('aaron@example.com', 'message');

expect(sendEmail).toHaveBeenCalledWith(
  'aaron@example.com',
  expect.stringContaining('message')
);
// Real email server never involved
```

## Spy — Wraps the Real Implementation

```typescript
const consoleSpy = jest.spyOn(console, 'error');
// console.error still runs (you see real output)
// but the spy records the calls

warnOnInvalidInput(null);

expect(consoleSpy).toHaveBeenCalledWith(
  expect.stringContaining('Invalid input')
);
consoleSpy.mockRestore(); // restore original
```

```typescript
// Spy with override (suppress the real behaviour)
const spy = jest.spyOn(api, 'fetchUser').mockResolvedValue({ name: 'Aaron' });
// Real fetchUser NOT called — spy overrides it
// But you can still restore the original:
spy.mockRestore();
```

## When to Use Each

| Scenario | Use |
|---|---|
| Replace a database call | Mock |
| Check if logger was called | Spy (let real logger run) |
| Mock an API in isolation | Mock |
| Verify a method on the real object was called | Spy |
| Suppress side effects (email, console) | Spy + mockImplementation |

## Summary

```
Mock:  replaces → real code never runs
Spy:   wraps   → real code runs + calls recorded
```

