## When to Mock

## Mock These

### External Services
```typescript
// Always mock HTTP calls in unit tests
jest.mock('@/lib/api', () => ({ fetchUser: jest.fn() }));
```

### Databases
```typescript
// Mock the DB layer in unit tests; use real DB in integration tests
const mockRepo = { findById: jest.fn().mockResolvedValue(user) };
```

### Time
```typescript
jest.useFakeTimers();
jest.setSystemTime(new Date('2024-01-15'));
// Now Date.now() is deterministic
```

### File System
```typescript
jest.mock('fs/promises', () => ({
  readFile: jest.fn().mockResolvedValue('file content'),
}));
```

## Don't Mock These

### Your Own Business Logic
```typescript
// ❌ Wrong: mocking the thing you're testing
jest.mock('./calculateDiscount');
// You've mocked the function being tested — the test is meaningless
```

### Simple Data Transformers
```typescript
// ❌ Don't mock formatDate — just call the real one
// formatDate is pure, fast, deterministic — no reason to mock
```

### Third-Party Libraries with Simple APIs
```typescript
// ❌ Don't mock lodash.cloneDeep — it's pure, fast, deterministic
// Only mock libraries with side effects or network calls
```

## Over-Mocking Smell

```typescript
// ❌ This test is brittle — it tests that mocks were called, not behaviour
jest.mock('./userService');
jest.mock('./emailService');
jest.mock('./auditLogger');

// If the real behavior matters, consider an integration test instead
```

## Rule of Thumb

Mock at the **boundary**: where your code touches the outside world. Don't mock the interior.

