## Code Coverage

## Types of Coverage

```
Line coverage:    % of lines executed
Branch coverage:  % of branches (if/else, ternary) executed
Function coverage: % of functions called
Statement coverage: % of statements executed
```

Branch coverage is the most useful — it catches untested conditional paths.

## Common Benchmarks

| Coverage | Assessment |
|---|---|
| < 50% | High risk — many untested paths |
| 50-70% | Acceptable for legacy/rapidly changing code |
| 70-80% | Solid baseline |
| 80-90% | Good — likely covers critical paths well |
| 90-95% | High — starting to test trivial code |
| 100% | Theoretical max — not worth chasing |

## The 80% Rule

The industry frequently cites 80% because the last 20% (trivial getters, error-handling branches, defensive checks) often costs more in test maintenance than it's worth.

## Coverage ≠ Quality

```typescript
// 100% coverage, zero value
test('does stuff', () => {
  const result = calculateTotal(items, discount, tax);
  expect(result).toBeDefined(); // just checks it ran — no assertion on value
});

// 60% coverage, high value
test('applies 20% discount correctly', () => {
  expect(calculateTotal([{ price: 100 }], 0.2, 0)).toBe(80);
});
test('throws if discount > 1', () => {
  expect(() => calculateTotal([], 1.5, 0)).toThrow();
});
```

## Practical Configuration

```javascript
// jest.config.js
coverageThreshold: {
  global: {
    branches: 80,
    functions: 85,
    lines: 85,
  },
}
```

